    "question_id": "rag_expl_06",
    "query": "How do validation curves diagnose whether a machine learning estimator is suffering from high bias versus high variance?",
    "reference_answer": "Validation curves plot training and test scores against a hyperparameter: if both scores are low the model has high bias (underfitting); if training score is high but validation score is substantially lower, the model has high variance (overfitting).",
    "generated_answer": "Validation curves diagnose bias versus variance by comparing the **training** and **validation** scores that are plotted for a range of hyper\u2011parameter values.  \n- **High bias (under\u2011fitting)**: both the training score and the validation score are low across the curve.  \n- **High variance (over\u2011fitting)**: the training score is high while the validation score is low.  \n- **Good fit**: both scores are high and close to each other.  \n\nThus, by inspecting whether the training and validation curves diverge or stay together, one can tell whether the estimator suffers from high bias or high variance. [Source 4]",
