    "query": "How do validation curves diagnose whether a machine learning estimator is suffering from high bias versus high variance?",
    "reference_answer": "Validation curves plot training and test scores against a hyperparameter: if both scores are low the model has high bias (underfitting); if training score is high but validation score is substantially lower, the model has high variance (overfitting).",
    "generated_answer": "Validation curves plot the **training score** and the **validation (cross\u2011validated) score** of an estimator as a function of a single hyper\u2011parameter.  \nBy inspecting the two curves you can tell whether the model is under\u2011 or over\u2011fitting:\n\n* **High bias (under\u2011fitting)** \u2013 both training and validation scores are low and close to each other.  \n* **High variance (over\u2011fitting)** \u2013 the training score is high while the validation score is noticeably lower, creating a large gap between the two curves.  \n\nThus, a validation curve diagnoses bias versus variance by showing how the training and validation performance change together as the hyper\u2011parameter is varied. [Source 4]",
