  {
    "question_id": "rag_expl_06",
    "query": "How do validation curves diagnose whether a machine learning estimator is suffering from high bias versus high variance?",
    "reference_answer": "Validation curves plot training and test scores against a hyperparameter: if both scores are low the model has high bias (underfitting); if training score is high but validation score is substantially lower, the model has high variance (overfitting).",
    "generated_answer": "Validation curves plot the **training score** and the **validation score** of an estimator as a function of a single hyper\u2011parameter.  \nBy inspecting the two curves you can tell whether the model is under\u2011 or over\u2011fitting:\n\n| Situation | Training score | Validation score | Interpretation |\n|-----------|----------------|------------------|----------------|\n| **High bias** | Low (close to the validation score) | Low | The model cannot fit the training data well; both scores are low and close together. |\n| **High variance** | High | Low (large gap) | The model fits the training data very well but generalises poorly; the training curve is high while the validation curve is low. |\n\nThus, a validation curve diagnoses bias versus variance by comparing how the training and validation scores behave across hyper\u2011parameter values.  \n[Source\u202f4]",
    "retrieved_chunk_ids": [
      "model_selection_curves_struct_0001",
      "model_selection_curves_struct_0004",
