  {
    "question_id": "rag_comp_01",
    "query": "What is the key difference between KFold and StratifiedKFold cross-validation for classification tasks?",
    "reference_answer": "KFold divides samples into k folds without regard to target class proportions, whereas StratifiedKFold preserves the percentage of samples for each class in every fold, preventing unrepresentative class distributions in classification tasks.",
    "generated_answer": "KFold simply partitions the data into \\(n_{\\text{splits}}\\) folds without regard to the target labels, so each fold can have a very different class mix.  \nStratifiedKFold, on the other hand, performs the split so that each fold contains roughly the same proportion of each class as the full dataset, preserving the class distribution in every train/test split.  \n\nThis stratification is especially important for classification tasks with imbalanced classes, where a non\u2011stratified split could leave some folds with very few or no samples of a minority class.  \n\n[Source\u202f1] [Source\u202f3]",
