  {
    "question_id": "rag_proc_14",
    "query": "How do you build a complete end-to-end composite pipeline that imputes missing values, scales features, and trains a LogisticRegression estimator?",
    "reference_answer": "Chain estimators sequentially: Pipeline([('imputer', SimpleImputer(strategy='mean')), ('scaler', StandardScaler()), ('clf', LogisticRegression())]) and call pipeline.fit(X_train, y_train).",
    "generated_answer": "To create an end\u2011to\u2011end pipeline that (1) imputes missing values, (2) scales the features, and (3) fits a `LogisticRegression`, you can chain the three estimators in a `Pipeline` (or use `make_pipeline`).  \nA typical construction is:\n\n```python\n# 1. Impute missing values\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer          # or IterativeImputer\n# If using IterativeImputer, enable it first:\n# from sklearn.experimental import enable_iterative_imputer\n# from sklearn.impute import IterativeImputer\n\n# 2. Scale the data\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\n\n# 3. Classifier\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression\n\n# Build the pipeline\npipe = Pipeline(steps=[\n    ('imputer', SimpleImputer()),          # or ('imputer', IterativeImputer())\n    ('scaler', StandardScaler()),\n    ('clf', LogisticRegression())\n])\n\n# Fit and evaluate\npipe.fit(X_train, y_train)\nscore = pipe.score(X_test, y_test)\n```\n\n- `Pipeline` exposes the methods of the last step (`LogisticRegression`), so `pipe.fit` and `pipe.score` work as expected.  \n- `SimpleImputer` (or `IterativeImputer`) can be placed as the first step to handle missing values before scaling.  \n- `StandardScaler` standardizes the features, and `LogisticRegression` trains the model.  \n\nThis follows the pattern described in the documentation: pipelines combine transformers (imputer, scaler) with a predictor (LogisticRegression) and expose the predictor\u2019s API [Source\u202f1], and the example of `StandardScaler` in a pipeline is shown in the docs [Source\u202f3]. The use of `IterativeImputer` is also supported in pipelines as noted in the imputation section [Source\u202f2].",
