  {
    "question_id": "rag_multi_09",
    "query": "How do preprocessing, pipelines, and model selection work together to build leak-free machine learning workflows in scikit-learn?",
    "reference_answer": "Preprocessing transformers (scalers, encoders, imputers) and models are chained in a Pipeline, and the entire pipeline is passed to model selection tools (GridSearchCV or cross_val_score); this ensures preprocessing parameters are estimated solely on training splits, eliminating data leakage.",
    "generated_answer": "Preprocessing, pipelines, and model\u2011selection tools in scikit\u2011learn are designed to be used together so that every step of a workflow is applied **only to the data that should influence the model** (the training set) and then reused unchanged on the test or validation data. The key ideas are:\n\n| Step | What it does | How it prevents leakage |\n|------|--------------|------------------------|\n| **Split first** | `train_test_split` (or a CV splitter) creates disjoint training and test sets. | Keeps the test data out of any fit operation. |\n| **Preprocess only on training data** | `fit`/`fit_transform` are called on the training set; `transform` is then applied to the test set. | The statistics (means, variances, selected features, etc.) are learned only from the training data, so the test data never influences the preprocessing. |\n| **Use a `Pipeline`** | A `Pipeline` chains transformers and a final estimator. Each step implements `fit` (and `transform` for non\u2011final steps). | When the pipeline\u2019s `fit` is called, it automatically calls `fit` on the first step, then `transform` on the next, and so on, ensuring the same sequence is applied to every split. This eliminates the risk of accidentally applying a transformation to the test set during training. |\n| **Handle heterogeneous data with `ColumnTransformer`** | Different columns can receive different preprocessing (e.g., `OneHotEncoder` for categorical, `StandardScaler` for numeric). | `ColumnTransformer` is itself a transformer that can be inserted into a pipeline, so its fit/transform logic is also confined to the training data. |\n| **Model selection / hyper\u2011parameter tuning** | `GridSearchCV`, `RandomizedSearchCV`, etc. wrap a pipeline and perform cross\u2011validation. | The CV splitter feeds each fold into the pipeline\u2019s `fit`, guaranteeing that every fold\u2019s training data is used to fit the preprocessing steps and that the corresponding test fold is only transformed, not used for fitting. |\n| **Caching (optional)** | `Pipeline(..., memory=\u2026)` caches fitted transformers. | Caching does not change the fact that the transformers are fitted only on the training data; it merely speeds up repeated calls. |\n\n**Putting it together**\n\n1. **Create a pipeline** that contains all preprocessing steps (e.g., `StandardScaler`, `SimpleImputer`, `SelectKBest`, `ColumnTransformer`) followed by the estimator (e.g., `RandomForestClassifier`).  \n2. **Wrap the pipeline in a model\u2011selection object** such as `GridSearchCV`.  \n3. **Fit the model\u2011selection object** on the full dataset. The CV splitter will split the data, the pipeline will fit the preprocessing on each training fold, transform the corresponding test fold, and then fit the estimator.  \n4. **Evaluate** on a held\u2011out test set that was never seen during any fit or transform step.\n\nBecause every transformation is performed inside the pipeline and every fit is confined to the training portion of",
