  {
    "question_id": "rag_trouble_01",
    "query": "Why does leaving random_state=None produce non-reproducible cross-validation results across multiple script executions?",
    "reference_answer": "When random_state=None (or np.random), cross-validation splitters and random estimators seed from system clock entropy, generating different random splits and parameter weights on every run. Fixing random_state to an integer ensures deterministic reproducibility.",
    "generated_answer": "Leaving `random_state=None` causes the estimator or CV splitter to use the global NumPy random state (`np.random.mtrand._rand`). Each time the object is fitted or split, it draws new random numbers, so the sequence of random choices (e.g., feature subsets, train/test splits) differs from run to run. Consequently, cross\u2011validation results are not reproducible across script executions.  \n- The documentation states that passing `None` (or a `RandomState` instance) makes `fit` and `split` yield different results each time they are called, exploring all sources of entropy [Source\u202f2].  \n- It also recommends removing any use of `random_state=None` to obtain reproducible results across executions [Source\u202f1].",
    "retrieved_chunk_ids": [
      "best_practices_common_pitfalls_struct_0006",
