  {
    "question_id": "rag_trouble_01",
    "query": "Why does leaving random_state=None produce non-reproducible cross-validation results across multiple script executions?",
    "reference_answer": "When random_state=None (or np.random), cross-validation splitters and random estimators seed from system clock entropy, generating different random splits and parameter weights on every run. Fixing random_state to an integer ensures deterministic reproducibility.",
    "generated_answer": "Leaving `random_state=None` makes the estimator or splitter use the global NumPy random generator (`np.random.mtrand._rand`). Each time the script runs, that generator has a different internal state, so the random draws (e.g., data shuffling, tree feature selection, weight initialization) differ. Consequently, the cross\u2011validation splits and the estimator\u2019s random initialization change on every execution, producing non\u2011reproducible results.  \n\n- `random_state=None` is the default and is equivalent to using the global RNG, which changes each run.  \n- When `None` (or a `RandomState` instance) is passed, `fit` or `split` will yield different results each time they are called, exploring all sources of entropy.  \n\nThus, to obtain reproducible cross\u2011validation results, one must avoid `random_state=None` and instead provide a fixed seed or a `RandomState` instance.  \n\n[Source 1] [Source 3]",
