    "generated_answer": "Feature selection with `SelectKBest` before calling `train_test_split` leaks information from the test set into the training process.  \n`SelectKBest` ranks features by their relationship with the target `y`. If you run it on the *entire* dataset, it uses the test labels to decide which features are \u201cbest\u201d. When the data are then split, the test set has already influenced the feature set that the model will train on, so the model is effectively trained on data that contains information about the test outcomes. This creates an optimistic bias: the model appears to perform well on the test set, but the reported error is severely underestimated because the test data were already used in feature selection.  \n\nThe correct practice is to split first, then fit `SelectKBest` only on the training data, and transform the test data with the fitted selector (or, even better, wrap the selector and estimator in a `Pipeline`) so that the test data remain unseen until evaluation.  \n\n[Source\u202f1] and [Source\u202f2] demonstrate this leakage and its effect on accuracy.",
